<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<lint>
    <!--
        ExpiredTargetSdkVersion and OldTargetApi are disabled on purpose: targetSdk 28 is intentional.
        The launcher is side-loaded on Android TV sets (it is not distributed through Google Play, so the
        Play target-API policy does not apply). The launcher runs the TV's Wi-Fi itself, and that needs
        the legacy WifiManager calls, which Android only keeps for apps targeting API 28 or lower
        ("Compatibility Note: for applications targeting Build.VERSION_CODES.Q or above, this API will
        always fail"):
          - WifiManager.setWifiEnabled (Wi-Fi switch),
          - WifiManager.addNetwork / enableNetwork / reconnect (joining, rejoining saved networks),
          - WifiManager.removeNetwork (Forget; only for networks the launcher saved itself),
          - WifiManager.getConfiguredNetworks (saved networks; needs location access).
        What else 28 means, checked:
          - runtime permissions still work as before (anything above 23 asks at runtime), so location
            and storage are still requested properly;
          - storage: no scoped storage below 29 (legacy view of shared storage, same READ grant);
          - package visibility: every package is visible below 30 (the <queries> block stays so a later
            raise keeps working);
          - Bluetooth: BLUETOOTH / BLUETOOTH_ADMIN install-time grants, discovery with location access;
          - ACTION_DELETE needs REQUEST_DELETE_PACKAGES from 28 (declared);
          - the notification listener and the dream service do not depend on the target.
        Raising targetSdk again requires moving Wi-Fi back to the TV's own settings first.
        See docs/SYSTEM_SETTINGS.md.
    -->
    <issue id="ExpiredTargetSdkVersion" severity="ignore" />
    <issue id="OldTargetApi" severity="ignore" />
</lint>
